1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,610
One hundred and eighteen billion dollars.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:05,798
That is roughly how much the United

3
00:00:05,798 --> 00:00:07,731
States Postal Service has lost since

4
00:00:07,731 --> 00:00:08,783
twenty oh six,

5
00:00:08,783 --> 00:00:11,790
according to the Government Accountability

6
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:12,820
Office.

7
00:00:13,083 --> 00:00:15,183
And it keeps going.

8
00:00:15,393 --> 00:00:17,565
The Postal Service lost nine point five

9
00:00:17,565 --> 00:00:20,196
billion dollars in twenty twenty-four,

10
00:00:20,196 --> 00:00:22,369
and nine billion dollars in twenty

11
00:00:22,369 --> 00:00:23,693
twenty-five.

12
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:26,176
On the day this video was made,

13
00:00:26,176 --> 00:00:28,251
it reported a loss of six point

14
00:00:28,251 --> 00:00:30,096
five billion dollars for twenty

15
00:00:30,096 --> 00:00:31,388
twenty-six.

16
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:34,523
So how does an organization that delivers

17
00:00:34,523 --> 00:00:37,618
to every address in America keep losing

18
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:38,660
money?

19
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:41,177
And who pays for it?

20
00:00:41,417 --> 00:00:43,298
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:43,298 --> 00:00:45,442
you will know what the Postal Service

22
00:00:45,442 --> 00:00:46,847
has to do by law,

23
00:00:46,847 --> 00:00:48,752
how a law from twenty oh six

24
00:00:48,752 --> 00:00:50,633
turned into a giant bill,

25
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,990
why the letter is disappearing,

26
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:55,134
and why the money is running out

27
00:00:55,134 --> 00:00:56,210
of room.

28
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:04,200
Start with the job.

29
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:06,721
The Postal Service delivers to more than

30
00:01:06,721 --> 00:01:09,536
one hundred and seventy million addresses,

31
00:01:09,536 --> 00:01:12,100
six and often seven days a week.

32
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:14,129
In twenty twenty-five alone,

33
00:01:14,129 --> 00:01:15,907
about one point eight million new

34
00:01:15,907 --> 00:01:18,730
addresses were added to its routes.

35
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:20,257
Every new house,

36
00:01:20,257 --> 00:01:22,141
every new apartment,

37
00:01:22,141 --> 00:01:23,695
is one more stop.

38
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:27,610
The machine behind that is enormous.

39
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,069
According to the Postal Service,

40
00:01:30,069 --> 00:01:32,646
it runs about two hundred and thirty-six

41
00:01:32,646 --> 00:01:34,730
thousand delivery routes,

42
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:37,518
nearly thirty-one thousand retail offices,

43
00:01:37,518 --> 00:01:39,158
and a fleet of more than two

44
00:01:39,158 --> 00:01:42,100
hundred and sixty thousand vehicles.

45
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:45,337
Every one of those vehicles has to

46
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:46,364
keep moving,

47
00:01:46,364 --> 00:01:48,807
whether the mail grows or not.

48
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,431
And here is the part many people

49
00:01:51,431 --> 00:01:52,560
get wrong.

50
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:55,200
The Postal Service generally receives no

51
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,190
tax dollars for its operating expenses.

52
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:00,385
It is supposed to pay its own

53
00:02:00,385 --> 00:02:00,834
way,

54
00:02:00,834 --> 00:02:01,991
by selling stamps,

55
00:02:01,991 --> 00:02:05,425
delivering packages and charging for mail.

56
00:02:05,683 --> 00:02:07,558
But it cannot run like a normal

57
00:02:07,558 --> 00:02:08,783
company.

58
00:02:08,893 --> 00:02:09,596
By law,

59
00:02:09,596 --> 00:02:11,778
it must deliver at least six days

60
00:02:11,778 --> 00:02:12,863
a week.

61
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:14,643
It must serve a farm at the

62
00:02:14,643 --> 00:02:16,234
end of a dirt road as well

63
00:02:16,234 --> 00:02:18,279
as an office tower in a big

64
00:02:18,279 --> 00:02:19,333
city.

65
00:02:19,463 --> 00:02:23,141
That promise is called universal service.

66
00:02:23,383 --> 00:02:25,926
That promise has a cost that does

67
00:02:25,926 --> 00:02:28,383
not shrink when the mail shrinks.

68
00:02:28,383 --> 00:02:30,280
A carrier still has to drive the

69
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,205
whole route,

70
00:02:31,205 --> 00:02:33,576
whether the bag holds fifty letters or

71
00:02:33,576 --> 00:02:34,333
five.

72
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:36,103
The network is fixed.

73
00:02:36,103 --> 00:02:38,332
The revenue is not.

74
00:02:38,583 --> 00:02:40,713
Let me draw the problem.

75
00:02:40,713 --> 00:02:43,283
Here is a route with ten houses.

76
00:02:43,283 --> 00:02:43,892
Each day,

77
00:02:43,892 --> 00:02:46,383
the carrier visits every one.

78
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:48,723
Now the letters fall by half.

79
00:02:48,723 --> 00:02:50,433
The route stays the same.

80
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:51,339
Same truck,

81
00:02:51,339 --> 00:02:52,558
same miles,

82
00:02:52,558 --> 00:02:53,783
same time.

83
00:02:53,783 --> 00:02:56,725
Fewer letters to pay for each stop.

84
00:02:56,983 --> 00:03:00,183
That alone would make life hard.

85
00:03:00,443 --> 00:03:02,237
But in twenty oh six,

86
00:03:02,237 --> 00:03:05,437
Congress added something much bigger.

87
00:03:16,083 --> 00:03:19,322
The Postal Accountability and Enhancement

88
00:03:19,322 --> 00:03:21,404
Act set fixed payments for the health

89
00:03:21,404 --> 00:03:23,623
care of future retirees.

90
00:03:24,023 --> 00:03:25,852
Every year for ten years,

91
00:03:25,852 --> 00:03:28,167
the Postal Service had to put roughly

92
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:30,250
five and a half billion dollars a

93
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:32,334
year into a fund for workers who

94
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:34,323
had not yet retired.

95
00:03:34,603 --> 00:03:35,520
By our sum,

96
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,340
about fifty-six billion dollars over the

97
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:39,410
decade.

98
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:40,999
Those were fixed amounts,

99
00:03:40,999 --> 00:03:42,349
written into the law,

100
00:03:42,349 --> 00:03:45,100
whatever happened to the business.

101
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:47,540
And they arrived just as the mail

102
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:49,660
started its long decline.

103
00:03:49,870 --> 00:03:52,342
The Postal Service soon stopped making

104
00:03:52,342 --> 00:03:52,812
them,

105
00:03:52,812 --> 00:03:55,283
and the missed payments kept piling up

106
00:03:55,283 --> 00:03:57,367
on its books as losses.

107
00:03:57,617 --> 00:03:59,537
In twenty twenty-two,

108
00:03:59,537 --> 00:04:01,707
Congress tried to fix it.

109
00:04:01,707 --> 00:04:04,200
The Postal Service Reform Act canceled

110
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,694
fifty-seven billion dollars of missed

111
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:07,747
payments.

112
00:04:07,987 --> 00:04:10,825
It also moved postal retirees onto

113
00:04:10,825 --> 00:04:11,747
Medicare,

114
00:04:11,747 --> 00:04:13,875
which cut the retiree health bill by

115
00:04:13,875 --> 00:04:16,367
about sixty-one billion dollars.

116
00:04:16,807 --> 00:04:19,268
And it wrote the six-day delivery rule

117
00:04:19,268 --> 00:04:21,098
directly into law.

118
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:23,643
But the reform did not end the

119
00:04:23,643 --> 00:04:24,500
bills.

120
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,015
The Postal Service still owes payments for

121
00:04:27,015 --> 00:04:27,678
pensions,

122
00:04:27,678 --> 00:04:29,965
and new top-up payments for retiree

123
00:04:29,965 --> 00:04:30,399
health,

124
00:04:30,399 --> 00:04:32,914
about seven hundred and fifty million

125
00:04:32,914 --> 00:04:34,949
dollars in twenty twenty-six,

126
00:04:34,949 --> 00:04:37,236
growing to about one point four billion

127
00:04:37,236 --> 00:04:39,271
by fiscal year twenty thirty-one,

128
00:04:39,271 --> 00:04:41,700
according to the auditors.

129
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:44,165
The government's auditors expect that

130
00:04:44,165 --> 00:04:46,010
health fund to run dry in fiscal

131
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:47,762
year twenty thirty-one.

132
00:04:48,017 --> 00:04:49,807
Let me draw the bill.

133
00:04:49,907 --> 00:04:52,047
Here is the Postal Service.

134
00:04:52,097 --> 00:04:55,157
Here is the fund for future retirees.

135
00:04:55,297 --> 00:04:56,164
Every year,

136
00:04:56,164 --> 00:04:58,112
a big arrow of money was supposed

137
00:04:58,112 --> 00:04:59,597
to flow into it.

138
00:04:59,907 --> 00:05:01,392
When the arrow stopped,

139
00:05:01,392 --> 00:05:02,637
the debt grew.

140
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:04,204
In twenty twenty-two,

141
00:05:04,204 --> 00:05:06,447
Congress erased much of it.

142
00:05:06,487 --> 00:05:08,954
But a smaller arrow is still there.

143
00:05:09,217 --> 00:05:11,984
The retiree bills explain part of the

144
00:05:11,984 --> 00:05:13,097
losses.

145
00:05:13,097 --> 00:05:15,580
The rest comes from something you can

146
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:17,671
see in your own mailbox.

147
00:05:23,317 --> 00:05:24,375
First-Class Mail,

148
00:05:24,375 --> 00:05:26,546
the letters and bills that once paid

149
00:05:26,546 --> 00:05:28,418
for the whole system,

150
00:05:28,418 --> 00:05:30,917
peaked in twenty oh one.

151
00:05:31,197 --> 00:05:31,763
That year,

152
00:05:31,763 --> 00:05:33,715
the Postal Service handled about one

153
00:05:33,715 --> 00:05:36,267
hundred and four billion pieces of it.

154
00:05:36,557 --> 00:05:37,954
In twenty twenty-six,

155
00:05:37,954 --> 00:05:40,377
it handled about forty billion.

156
00:05:40,577 --> 00:05:42,325
Counting every kind of mail,

157
00:05:42,325 --> 00:05:44,465
the total peaked a few years later,

158
00:05:44,465 --> 00:05:45,624
in twenty oh six,

159
00:05:45,624 --> 00:05:47,391
at more than two hundred and thirteen

160
00:05:47,391 --> 00:05:48,777
billion pieces.

161
00:05:48,837 --> 00:05:51,310
It has been falling ever since.

162
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:54,290
Here is the most striking number.

163
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:56,163
In twenty oh seven,

164
00:05:56,163 --> 00:05:58,898
each delivery point received about five

165
00:05:58,898 --> 00:06:00,722
and a half pieces of mail a

166
00:06:00,722 --> 00:06:01,550
day.

167
00:06:01,730 --> 00:06:03,609
In twenty twenty-six,

168
00:06:03,609 --> 00:06:05,570
it was two point three.

169
00:06:05,910 --> 00:06:06,703
More stops,

170
00:06:06,703 --> 00:06:08,616
and much less in the bag at

171
00:06:08,616 --> 00:06:09,763
each one.

172
00:06:10,017 --> 00:06:12,617
Packages are the bright spot.

173
00:06:12,717 --> 00:06:14,398
In twenty twenty-six,

174
00:06:14,398 --> 00:06:16,347
shipping and packages brought in

175
00:06:16,347 --> 00:06:18,759
thirty-four point one billion dollars,

176
00:06:18,759 --> 00:06:21,195
more than the twenty-six point two billion

177
00:06:21,195 --> 00:06:23,257
dollars from First-Class Mail.

178
00:06:23,407 --> 00:06:24,958
In twenty twenty-five,

179
00:06:24,958 --> 00:06:27,205
the Postal Service said its revenue grew

180
00:06:27,205 --> 00:06:29,228
mostly thanks to its Ground Advantage

181
00:06:29,228 --> 00:06:30,727
shipping service.

182
00:06:31,097 --> 00:06:34,177
But packages are a competitive business.

183
00:06:34,317 --> 00:06:36,179
Private carriers fight for the same

184
00:06:36,179 --> 00:06:37,087
customers,

185
00:06:37,087 --> 00:06:39,647
so the Postal Service cannot simply raise

186
00:06:39,647 --> 00:06:40,713
prices.

187
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:42,024
On letters,

188
00:06:42,024 --> 00:06:43,923
it has raised prices,

189
00:06:43,923 --> 00:06:45,900
again and again.

190
00:06:45,950 --> 00:06:48,329
A Forever stamp cost fifty-five cents in

191
00:06:48,329 --> 00:06:50,010
twenty nineteen.

192
00:06:50,250 --> 00:06:52,687
Since July twenty twenty-six,

193
00:06:52,687 --> 00:06:54,930
it costs eighty-two cents.

194
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:56,548
By our calculation,

195
00:06:56,548 --> 00:06:58,920
that is about forty-nine percent more in

196
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,167
seven years.

197
00:07:00,417 --> 00:07:02,987
Let me draw the two lines.

198
00:07:02,987 --> 00:07:04,792
Here is the letter line,

199
00:07:04,792 --> 00:07:07,147
starting high in twenty oh one and

200
00:07:07,147 --> 00:07:09,317
falling year after year.

201
00:07:09,447 --> 00:07:11,383
Here is the stamp price line,

202
00:07:11,383 --> 00:07:13,667
starting low and climbing.

203
00:07:13,677 --> 00:07:16,129
When fewer people buy something,

204
00:07:16,129 --> 00:07:18,357
raising the price only works for so

205
00:07:18,357 --> 00:07:19,205
long.

206
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:21,063
In twenty twenty-one,

207
00:07:21,063 --> 00:07:24,069
the Postal Service announced a plan to

208
00:07:24,069 --> 00:07:26,309
turn all of this around.

209
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:35,468
It was called Delivering for America,

210
00:07:35,468 --> 00:07:37,080
a ten-year plan.

211
00:07:37,190 --> 00:07:39,584
It promised to break even by fiscal

212
00:07:39,584 --> 00:07:41,086
year twenty twenty-three,

213
00:07:41,086 --> 00:07:43,263
and to avoid one hundred and sixty

214
00:07:43,263 --> 00:07:45,875
billion dollars in projected losses over

215
00:07:45,875 --> 00:07:46,910
ten years.

216
00:07:47,090 --> 00:07:49,819
It also promised forty billion dollars of

217
00:07:49,819 --> 00:07:52,938
investment and better on-time delivery.

218
00:07:53,183 --> 00:07:56,093
Break-even never came.

219
00:07:56,193 --> 00:07:58,503
The government's auditors found that from

220
00:07:58,503 --> 00:08:01,020
twenty twenty-one to twenty twenty-four,

221
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:03,561
revenue was actually about twenty-four

222
00:08:03,561 --> 00:08:05,640
billion dollars higher than the plan

223
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:06,933
expected.

224
00:08:07,233 --> 00:08:09,926
But expenses were about thirty-six billion

225
00:08:09,926 --> 00:08:11,423
dollars higher.

226
00:08:11,683 --> 00:08:15,012
The costs ran faster than the sales.

227
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:18,299
The twenty twenty-six loss looked smaller,

228
00:08:18,299 --> 00:08:20,180
but read the fine print.

229
00:08:20,270 --> 00:08:21,970
Much of the improvement came from

230
00:08:21,970 --> 00:08:24,030
accounting for workers' compensation,

231
00:08:24,030 --> 00:08:26,330
which moves with interest rates.

232
00:08:26,530 --> 00:08:28,599
That one line swung by about two

233
00:08:28,599 --> 00:08:30,750
point four billion dollars.

234
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:33,005
The loss the Postal Service says it

235
00:08:33,005 --> 00:08:35,255
can actually control was still more than

236
00:08:35,255 --> 00:08:36,980
two billion dollars.

237
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:38,580
In twenty twenty-five,

238
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,907
that controllable loss was about two point

239
00:08:40,907 --> 00:08:42,353
seven billion.

240
00:08:42,617 --> 00:08:44,487
Let me draw the gap.

241
00:08:44,667 --> 00:08:47,467
Here is the revenue the plan expected.

242
00:08:47,917 --> 00:08:49,729
Here is what actually came in,

243
00:08:49,729 --> 00:08:51,347
a little higher.

244
00:08:51,737 --> 00:08:54,099
Now here are the expected costs,

245
00:08:54,099 --> 00:08:55,931
and here are the real costs,

246
00:08:55,931 --> 00:08:57,327
much higher.

247
00:08:57,657 --> 00:08:59,970
The plan beat its sales target and

248
00:08:59,970 --> 00:09:00,716
still lost,

249
00:09:00,716 --> 00:09:03,629
because the costs grew even faster.

250
00:09:03,883 --> 00:09:06,346
Which leads to the most serious problem

251
00:09:06,346 --> 00:09:07,493
of all.

252
00:09:07,563 --> 00:09:10,199
The Postal Service is running out of

253
00:09:10,199 --> 00:09:11,853
room to borrow.

254
00:09:17,517 --> 00:09:19,876
The Postal Service can borrow from the

255
00:09:19,876 --> 00:09:20,796
Treasury,

256
00:09:20,796 --> 00:09:23,156
but only up to a legal limit

257
00:09:23,156 --> 00:09:25,407
of fifteen billion dollars.

258
00:09:25,547 --> 00:09:28,106
That limit has not changed since nineteen

259
00:09:28,106 --> 00:09:29,637
ninety-two.

260
00:09:29,767 --> 00:09:32,034
And the Postal Service has already reached

261
00:09:32,034 --> 00:09:32,823
it.

262
00:09:33,083 --> 00:09:35,521
So in twenty twenty-six,

263
00:09:35,521 --> 00:09:37,973
it started skipping bills.

264
00:09:38,153 --> 00:09:40,304
With a waiver from its regulator,

265
00:09:40,304 --> 00:09:42,081
it did not make its pension payments

266
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:43,273
for the year.

267
00:09:43,663 --> 00:09:45,550
It paused some employer pension

268
00:09:45,550 --> 00:09:47,093
contributions.

269
00:09:47,433 --> 00:09:48,852
And in its own words,

270
00:09:48,852 --> 00:09:51,499
it defaulted on a seven hundred and

271
00:09:51,499 --> 00:09:53,905
twenty-six million dollar payment for

272
00:09:53,905 --> 00:09:55,467
retiree health.

273
00:09:55,717 --> 00:09:57,677
The long-term gaps are huge.

274
00:09:57,677 --> 00:10:00,927
The Postal Service reports ninety-seven

275
00:10:00,927 --> 00:10:03,635
point one billion dollars in unfunded

276
00:10:03,635 --> 00:10:05,504
pension obligations,

277
00:10:05,504 --> 00:10:08,483
and about forty-four billion dollars more

278
00:10:08,483 --> 00:10:10,437
for retiree health.

279
00:10:10,627 --> 00:10:13,112
The government's auditors have kept its

280
00:10:13,112 --> 00:10:15,349
finances on their high-risk list since

281
00:10:15,349 --> 00:10:16,566
twenty oh nine,

282
00:10:16,566 --> 00:10:18,906
and say Congress should act now.

283
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:21,790
Let me draw the squeeze.

284
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:22,782
On one side,

285
00:10:22,782 --> 00:10:26,150
a fixed network that grows every year.

286
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:27,156
On another,

287
00:10:27,156 --> 00:10:28,950
letters that keep falling.

288
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:29,796
On top,

289
00:10:29,796 --> 00:10:33,140
retiree bills that never go away.

290
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:34,344
And underneath,

291
00:10:34,344 --> 00:10:37,400
a credit line already at its limit.

292
00:10:37,570 --> 00:10:38,534
Four walls,

293
00:10:38,534 --> 00:10:40,797
closing in at once.

294
00:10:41,050 --> 00:10:42,963
So why does the Post Office keep

295
00:10:42,963 --> 00:10:44,470
losing billions?

296
00:10:44,470 --> 00:10:46,287
Because it is asked to run like

297
00:10:46,287 --> 00:10:48,305
a business and serve like a public

298
00:10:48,305 --> 00:10:50,520
utility at the same time.

299
00:10:50,670 --> 00:10:52,956
It must reach every address,

300
00:10:52,956 --> 00:10:54,215
six days a week,

301
00:10:54,215 --> 00:10:56,784
while its most profitable product

302
00:10:56,784 --> 00:10:59,866
disappears and old promises to retirees

303
00:10:59,866 --> 00:11:00,980
come due.

304
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:03,605
Until Congress decides who pays for that

305
00:11:03,605 --> 00:11:04,281
promise,

306
00:11:04,281 --> 00:11:06,280
the losses are the bill.

307
00:11:06,510 --> 00:11:07,534
Every stamp,

308
00:11:07,534 --> 00:11:10,251
every package and every new address on

309
00:11:10,251 --> 00:11:11,714
a route is part of that same

310
00:11:11,714 --> 00:11:13,150
calculation.

311
00:11:13,150 --> 00:11:14,142
And for now,

312
00:11:14,142 --> 00:11:15,897
the bill keeps arriving,

313
00:11:15,897 --> 00:11:17,515
six days a week.

314
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:20,067
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

315
00:11:20,067 --> 00:11:22,830
we follow the money behind another hidden

316
00:11:22,830 --> 00:11:25,120
link of the world economy.

317
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:27,650
This is The Hidden Economics.
